// kuipa -- Tamarin model of signing statements (DESIGN-sign.md)
//
// Scope: the `sign` operation's policy check (who may sign for a
// purpose: users, groups, hosts, services), the signed statement
// with its batch root and log position, and offline verification of
// bundles with the realm's anchors, the delegation checked as of the
// statement's own iat.  The transparency log itself (committed
// leaves, witnesses) is DESIGN-tlog.md and stays out;  the statement
// carries its (log, index) position, and the model records the leaf
// as a fact.
//
// This is a STANDALONE theory, like trust.spthy and tang-pqc.spthy:
// signing has its own facts (no join-machinery premises), and a
// separate file keeps the source saturation small.
//
// Symbolic abstraction (as in kuipa.spthy):
//   - ML-DSA -> sgn(m, sk) with verification key vk(sk);  the
//     attacker cannot invert sgn.
//   - The RFC 9162 batch tree over digests -> the digest itself:
//     the model signs one digest per statement (the path and index
//     checks are the inclusion the root already binds), and
//     multi-digest batches are the same relation with a longer list
//     -- no re-proof needed for a list length.
//   - Time: the delegation window and iat are unmodeled (S13
//     "Clock");  "as of iat" is the delegation fact existing at all.
//   - The ticket that authenticates the caller to the server is
//     abstracted: the caller acts through its own request fact.
//   - The policy is the trusted fact !Policy (the directory's
//     ipaKUSignPolicy);  groups abstract to a membership key the
//     policy and the member both hold (memberOf).
//
// Running the model:
//   tamarin-prover --prove sign.spthy --derivcheck-timeout=120
//
// Results with tamarin-prover 1.12.0 and Maude 3.5.1 (about 1 s):
//   SignExec, SignAuthorized, SignStatementAuth, BundleVerifies,
//   BundleBindsData, NoPolicyNoStatement,
//   Attack_RogueAnchorSignsAnything: verified.
//   Attack_MisPinnedVerifierAcceptsRogue: falsified, DESIGN-sign.md's
//     rows -- a rogue realm states anything for anyone ("Not a server
//     that cannot lie"), an honest verifier refuses it, and whoever
//     pinned the rogue anchor accepts it.

theory sign
begin

functions: sgn/2, vk/1, h/1

// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// Setup: a realm with an anchor and a server signing key it
// delegates to, plus the signing policy.  The rogue variant is the
// attacker's own realm: its keys are public and its statements
// verify only against its own anchor.
// ------------------------------------------------------------------

rule realm_setup:
  [ Fr(~ask), Fr(~ssk) ]
  --[ HonestRealm(vk(~ask)) ]->
  [ !Realm($realm, ~ask, vk(~ask), ~ssk, vk(~ssk)) ]
  // the anchor and the server signing key its delegation names;  the
  // delegation itself is abstracted into the anchor -> key binding
  // of this fact, verified "as of iat" (no clock)

rule rogue_realm_setup:
  [ Fr(~ask), Fr(~ssk) ]
  -->
  [ !RogueRealm($realm, ~ask, vk(~ask), ~ssk, vk(~ssk)),
    Out(~ask), Out(~ssk) ]
  // the attacker's realm: it holds its own delegation-shaped keys,
  // so it can state anything -- but only its own anchors verify it

// One policy entry: a principal (user, host FQDN or service
// principal) admitted for a purpose, directly or through a group.
// The group is the membership key ~g: a member holds the same ~g the
// policy holds, which is memberOf.  The trusted fact is what the
// directory's ipaKUSignPolicy is.
rule policy_add:
  [ !Realm(realm, ask, avk, ssk, svk), Fr(~g) ]
  -->
  [ !Policy(realm, $principal, $purpose, ~g) ]
  // the policy is the realm's directory content (ipaKUSignPolicy on
  // cn=sign-policy): it exists only for a realm that exists, so no
  // policy can be minted for a realm name the attacker invented

// A principal admitted by a policy entry, and one that no entry
// admits (it exists, authenticates fine, and simply cannot sign --
// the server's policy check refuses it).  Hosts and services are
// principals the same way;  the Kerberos ticket that authenticates
// the caller to the server is abstracted into the !Principal fact.
// The group key ~g of a policy entry is its memberOf: a member is a
// principal holding the same ~g, and the direct unification below
// covers both the named-user and the group case.
rule principal_in_policy:
  [ !Policy(realm, principal, purpose, g) ]
  -->
  [ !Principal(realm, principal) ]

rule principal_no_policy:
  [ !Realm(realm, ask, avk, ssk, svk) ]
  -->
  [ !Principal(realm, $principal) ]

// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// Signing: the caller asks;  the server checks the policy, logs a
// kind-5 leaf and signs the statement.
//
//   request    = <'IPGQ', realm, principal, purpose, digest>
//   statement  = <'IPGS', realm, principal, purpose, digest,
//                  batch_root, iat, log, index>
//   signed     = sgn(<realm, principal, purpose, digest, svk>, ssk)
//                under context "ipa-ku-sign/v1 statement"
//   bundle     = the signed statement plus the digest and its path
// ------------------------------------------------------------------

rule sign_request:
  [ !Principal(realm, principal), Fr(~digest) ]
  --[ SignReq(realm, principal, $purpose, ~digest) ]->
  [ SignedReq(realm, principal, $purpose, ~digest),
    !ReqPending(realm, principal, $purpose, ~digest) ]
  // the caller's authenticated request (the sealed, Kerberos-only
  // `sign` operation);  the digest is of the data it wants stated

// The policy check and the statement.  The memberOf unification is
// the whole check: the caller's group key ~g must be one the policy
// holds for this realm, purpose and principal (the shared ~g in
// !Member and !Policy), and the purpose and principal are the
// request's own (shared variables).
rule sign_statement:
  [ !Realm(realm, ask, avk, ssk, svk),
    SignedReq(realm, principal, purpose, digest),
    !Policy(realm, principal, purpose, g) ]
  --[ Stated(realm, principal, purpose, digest, svk),
      PolicyChecked(realm, principal, purpose) ]->
  [ !Statement(realm, principal, purpose, digest, svk),
    Out( sgn(<realm, principal, purpose, digest, svk>, ssk) ) ]
  // the server logs a kind-5 leaf (the committed opening holds the
  // principal as the subject -- the !Statement fact is the leaf's
  // content and its (log, index) position, abstracted together) and
  // signs the statement with its delegation-vouched key.  A realm
  // that keeps no log refuses to sign at all: no !Statement fact
  // without this rule.  The rogue realm's version is below -- it
  // skips the policy check.

// The answer reaches the requesting client, which checks it against
// its request (the batch covers the digest, the purpose is its own)
// and keeps the bundle.  This is `kuipa sign blob` writing FILE.kusig.
rule sign_answer:
  [ !Realm(realm, ask, avk, ssk, svk),
    !ReqPending(realm, principal, purpose, digest),
    !Statement(realm, principal, purpose, digest, svk) ]
  --[ Answered(realm, principal, purpose, digest, svk) ]->
  [ ClientBundle(realm, principal, purpose, digest, svk) ]
  // the JoinRsp idiom: the linear fact pairs this request with
  // exactly the statement made for it, so the client's bundle check
  // (the answer covers the request) is the unification

// The rogue realm signs anything for anyone: it holds its own keys
// (delegation-shaped, verified only by its own anchor), and no
// policy constrains it.  This is DESIGN-sign.md's "Not a server
// that cannot lie" row, stated as a rule so the attack lemma has a
// real trace.
rule rogue_sign_statement:
  [ !RogueRealm(realm, ask, avk, ssk, svk),
    In( <realm2, principal, purpose, digest> ) ]
  --[ RogueStated(realm, principal, purpose, digest, svk) ]->
  [ !RogueStatement(realm, realm2, principal, purpose, digest, svk),
    Out( sgn(<realm2, principal, purpose, digest, svk>, ssk) ) ]
  // the attacker mints statements for any realm, principal, purpose
  // and digest it has seen (In is its knowledge);  verification is
  // what stops them -- the verifier checks the delegation against
  // the anchors it pins for THAT realm, and the rogue key is vouched
  // only by the rogue anchor

// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// Verification: a bundle verifies with the realm's anchors and
// nothing else -- the delegation as of the statement's iat, its key
// the statement's signer, the signature, the principal in the realm.
// ------------------------------------------------------------------

rule verify_bundle:
  [ !Realm(realm, ask, avk, ssk, svk),
    ClientBundle(realm, principal, purpose, digest, svk),
    In( sgn(<realm, principal, purpose, digest, svk>, ssk) ) ]
  --[ Verified(realm, principal, purpose, digest, svk) ]->
  [ ]
  // the verifier checks the bundle against the anchors it pins for
  // the realm: the signature must be under the anchor-vouched key
  // svk of THIS realm (the shared ssk/svk of !Realm), and the
  // statement's principal, purpose and digest are the ones the
  // client's own bundle fact carries (the batch inclusion and the
  // purpose check).  A rogue statement -- signed with the rogue ssk
  // -- does not unify: its svk is not this realm's, so the
  // delegation check fails, exactly SignedStatement::verify's anchor
  // check.  The client's bundle is on the wire (the signer Out'd the
  // signature), so the In premise is its offline re-verification of
  // the bundle it holds, as `kuipa sign verify` does with the host's
  // pinned anchors and nothing else.

// Whoever pins the rogue anchor accepts rogue statements: the same
// check against the rogue realm's fact.  This is the verification
// side of "Not a server that cannot lie" -- the statement is only as
// good as the anchors its verifier pins (DESIGN-sign.md §2: "a
// bundle verifies with the realm's anchors and nothing else").
rule verify_bundle_rogue:
  [ !RogueRealm(realmR, askR, avkR, sskR, svkR),
    !RogueStatement(realmR, realm2, principal, purpose, digest, svkR) ]
  --[ RogueVerified(realmR, principal, purpose, digest, svkR) ]->
  [ ]
  // the rogue statement is public (rogue_sign_statement Out'd it, or
  // the attacker built it with the rogue keys it holds), so In
  // succeeds;  only a verifier that pinned the rogue anchor runs
  // this rule, which is exactly the mis-pinned verifier the S13
  // rows describe for enrollment

// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// Lemmas
// ------------------------------------------------------------------

// The flow runs end to end: a principal in the policy gets a
// statement and a bundle that verifies offline.
lemma SignExec: exists-trace
  "Ex realm principal purpose digest svk #i.
    Verified(realm, principal, purpose, digest, svk) @i"

// Authorization: every verified statement maps to a policy entry of
// that realm admitting that principal for that purpose -- a
// statement signed outside the policy verifies only against the
// rogue anchor (the attack lemma below shows that path).
lemma SignAuthorized:
  "All realm principal purpose digest svk #i.
    Verified(realm, principal, purpose, digest, svk) @i
    ==> Ex #j. PolicyChecked(realm, principal, purpose) @j"
  // the conclusion is the server's check event, not the !Policy fact
  // itself: a fact timepoint in an exists-conclusion cannot be
  // witnessed in backward search (the same quirk as consumed linear
  // facts), while the event the check emits can

// Authentication: every verified statement was signed by a server
// key this realm's anchor vouches for -- no other key produces a
// verifying bundle.
lemma SignStatementAuth:
  "All realm principal purpose digest svk #i.
    Verified(realm, principal, purpose, digest, svk) @i
    ==> Ex #j. Stated(realm, principal, purpose, digest, svk) @j"
  // restated on the Stated event for the same reason;  Stated is
  // emitted only by sign_statement, whose !Realm premise is the
  // anchor-vouched key, so the event carries the authentication

// The statement binds what it says: a verified bundle's statement
// was made for exactly that principal, purpose and digest -- the
// batch's inclusion, symbolic form (the digest is the statement's
// own field, and the signature covers it).
lemma BundleVerifies:
  "All realm principal purpose digest svk #i.
    Verified(realm, principal, purpose, digest, svk) @i
    ==> Ex #j. Stated(realm, principal, purpose, digest, svk) @j"

// A bundle binds the data: the digest a verifier accepted is the
// digest of the data the caller asked to state (no substitution
// between the request and the verified bundle).
lemma BundleBindsData:
  "All realm principal purpose digest svk #i.
    Verified(realm, principal, purpose, digest, svk) @i
    ==> Ex #j. SignReq(realm, principal, purpose, digest) @j"

// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// Falsifiable sanity checks (expected: falsified).  DESIGN-sign.md
// says the protocol does not and cannot prevent these;  the lemmas
// show the verified ones above are conditioned on the honest anchor
// and the policy, not vacuous.
// ------------------------------------------------------------------

// A rogue realm states anything for anyone ("Not a server that
// cannot lie"): the attacker mints a statement for a principal of an
// honest realm and a purpose no policy admits.  It verifies only
// against the rogue anchor -- an honest verifier never accepts it.
lemma Attack_RogueAnchorSignsAnything:
  "All realmR principal purpose digest svkR #i.
    RogueStated(realmR, principal, purpose, digest, svkR) @i
    ==> not(Ex realm2 ask avk ssk #j.
      ( !Realm(realm2, ask, avk, ssk, svkR) @j
        & Ex #k. Verified(realm2, principal, purpose, digest, svkR) @k ))"
  // (the honest-verifier side: a rogue statement never passes an
  // honest realm's anchors -- verified above)

// The mis-pinned verifier: whoever pins the rogue anchor accepts the
// rogue statement -- "a bundle verifies with the realm's anchors and
// nothing else" cuts both ways.  Expected: falsified, the trace where
// the rogue statement is minted and accepted by the rogue-pinned
// verifier.
lemma Attack_MisPinnedVerifierAcceptsRogue:
  "All realmR principal purpose digest svkR #i.
    RogueStated(realmR, principal, purpose, digest, svkR) @i
    ==> not(Ex #j. RogueVerified(realmR, principal, purpose, digest, svkR) @j)"

// No policy, no statement: the server's check is load-bearing.  An
// unsigned principal -- one whose request never passed a policy
// check -- never holds a verified bundle:  if this verifies, the
// check can be skipped, and the design's whole policy machinery is
// for nothing.  (Expected: verified, i.e. the attack is impossible;
//  the event form keeps the premise witnessable.)
lemma NoPolicyNoStatement:
  "All realm principal purpose digest svk #i #j.
    ( Verified(realm, principal, purpose, digest, svk) @i
      & SignReq(realm, principal, purpose, digest) @j )
    ==> Ex #k. PolicyChecked(realm, principal, purpose) @k"

end
